  {
    "episode_id": 43939,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/3/43939.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, so even before I got to the assembly, when I was an NRDC water and fish attorney, I was involved in a huge river restoration project, the San Joaquin River, third largest river in the state, second longest river, but in the 1940s, with the completion of a huge federal dam, the Bryant Dam, they literally dried up the entire river, took all the water, left a 60 mile stretch that was just sand. It's one of the most glaring just abuses, I think, of environmental values and impacts to downstream interests that we've seen with a large water project. So for about 20 years, this coalition of environmental and fishing groups litigated against the federal government and the water contractors there. And I got to work on bringing that longstanding litigation to a settlement that actually worked for both the farmers, the government, and the environmental and fishing coalition. It's, I think, a pretty good success story. And we have returned flows and salmon, believe it or not, to this dead river. And we've done it in a way that is respectful and workable for the ag interests involved in the San Joaquin Valley.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
