  {
    "episode_id": 43939,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/3/43939.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, thanks for asking about that. So this is an issue that to a lot of folks who depend on eel river diversions and basically I'm talking about anybody. in the Russian River Basin downstream of Lake Mendocino, and also the folks in Potter Valley. It's really scary because for the last 100 years, those diversions through PG&E's hydro project have been kind of a lifeline. It's most of the water that fills up Lake Mendocino in any given year, and it's all of the water that comes through the Potter Valley. So the idea of that project going away, which is what is happening, PG&E is abandoning the project, for economic reasons largely. They lose, you know, six to seven million dollars a year on this project. So they're doing what we call abandonment and decommissioning. That's a process that'll be overseen by the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission, FERC. And, you know, obviously that's a pretty scary scenario for a lot of folks. But I think there is an opportunity here because this project has never been all that valuable for hydro power. It is obviously very valuable for water. And there is a way to manage it to be a win-win where we continue to get those essential diversions into the Russian River Basin, probably in a way where they would come mostly in wet winter months and not so much in the summer. And you can do that using a run of the river diversion that would involve removing Scott Dam over on the other side, on the Eel River side, And that opens up all kinds of salmon and fish habitat restoration that's super important to tribes, to Humboldt County, to the fishing interests. All of them are engaged in the FERC proceeding and everybody's lawyered up. And my, what I've been trying to do, Dan, for the last three years is get folks to the table to agree on a win-win, where yes, we'll give you a free-flowing river on the eel. but we're going to have a run of the river diversion sufficient to meet the needs in the Russian river basin. That's totally doable. We've done the studies, the modeling. We can do that, and that'll provide long-term certainty for the folks on the Russian river side and really a big and exciting environmental restoration opportunity on the eel. Here's the thing that people need to understand. For the first 15 years of this project's life, back in the early 1900s, there was no Scott Dam. It was a run of the river hydro diversion, just like what we're proposing right now. And guess what? During that 15-year period, more water was diverted into the Russian River Basin than we've been diverting in recent years. So anyone who says, gosh, you can't do it. You can't get there. We've got a 15-year record from the early life of this project that proves you can do it. We can absolutely do it. I hope we can get folks to work together toward that win-win outcome. That has been my two-basin solution. Right now, it's sort of devolved into fighting and name-calling, and I'm certainly getting called my share of names by folks who I think are misrepresenting what's going on or maybe not understanding. But I'm convinced that at the end of the day, we will bring this thing together around this very concept of a two-basin solution, and it's going to work very well for Mendocino County.",
