  {
    "episode_id": 43939,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/3/43939.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 36,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, it's obviously a scary time. This Supreme Court is radical. They are not judicial conservatives. They do not practice judicial constraint. They have an agenda. And they've been building to this moment now for 30 almost 40 years. This did not just happen. These are right wing groups in particular, right wing evangelical, white Christian nationalist groups that have been working to get a court like this. And they finally got it. And that road decision, the Dobbs decision is maybe the biggest gut punch, but it wasn't the only one. We had a series of terrible decisions, this term from this radical Supreme Court. In the case of West Virginia versus EPA, they literally just made up a new legal doctrine to stop EPA from regulating the power sector from basically regulating coal out of existence. And it's a so-called major questions doctrine where they've said if an agency tries to pass regulations that have an economy-wide impact, and implicate that kind of a major question. There has to be explicit congressional authorization for them to do the exact thing they're trying to do. Otherwise, they can't do it. That is just something they made up out of whole cloth. That is nowhere in the Clean Air Act. It's nowhere in federal law or the Constitution. So anyone who thinks that judicial conservatives don't engage in activism and pursue radical agendas, just take a look at that. And then they've also done some really disturbing things on religious liberty. You know, our First Amendment provides two pieces of religious liberty. One is the establishment clause that protects us from our government mandating religion or explicitly endorsing or recognizing any religion. That's very important, the separation of church and state. the freedom from religion being imposed on you or being adopted by your government. Then the second part is the free exercise. You are free to do anything you want in your individual life. Well, the court has kind of exalted the free exercise side of that in a way. that all but nullifies the establishment clause. So the state of Maine, in one of the cases this term, was contracting out some of its education services in rural areas where they didn't have public schools. So they We're paying private entities to basically do charter schools and churches sued saying that they were discriminating by not allowing churches to compete for those education contracts. And Maine has long had a long standing policy like many states against not publicly funding church schools. Well, this Supreme Court said you now have to do that. So in other words, by honoring the establishment clause and defending the separation of church and state, the state of Maine in this Supreme Court's eyes violated the free exercise clause. That's how twisted this religious agenda of theirs has become. And then of course they had a horrible case about a football coach who was at least indirectly pressuring athletes to join him at midfield in prayer. And the school district was told they couldn't do anything about that. They had to let that happen in the name of free exercise. So it's pretty disturbing.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
