  {
    "episode_id": 43939,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/3/43939.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 40,
    "turn_text": "Well, I am getting more optimistic every day, Dan. And I mean literally every day yesterday in the state of Alaska, for the first time in 40 years, Alaskans elected a Democrat to represent them in Congress. It's a Native American pro-choice woman named Mary Foltola. She's going to be sworn in to be my colleague in Congress next week. And it is now the third special election that we were supposed to lose that we want. Our turnout is off the charts, Democratic and independent voters and even a lot of Republicans who care about women's rights and reproductive choice are coming to the polls and they're voting. You saw what happened in Kansas. So something's going on. And I think it bodes very well for our chances of hanging on to our majority and if not growing it.",
