    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/3/43939.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 46,
    "turn_text": "I think the only remaining big thing you're going to see in Congress before the election is a fight in the weeks ahead over this side deal that Joe Manchin cut with Chuck Schumer. And folks know we got the inflation reduction act. I supported it. It wasn't ideal. There were some bad fossil fuel businesses usual in it, but some really great industrial policy reforms that are going to move us to clean energy. And I welcome the chance to vote for that. But this side deal that Manchin cut with Schumer is not binding on me or anyone else. And it's about so-called permitting reform. Manchin wants to make it really easy to do massive fossil fuel projects. And so he's saying, let's put the fossil in with the wind and solar and everything else and just make it really easy to shortcut environmental reviews. I'm not going to support that kind of policy. I'm happy to have the conversation about clean energy and transmission lines, but not for fossil fuel pipelines and LNG facilities, no way. And we're going to have a big fight about that when I get back to Congress in the weeks ahead because Chuck Schumer feels like he needs to honor that deal. I feel like I own Joe Manchin, absolutely nothing. So that's probably something you'll be reading more about. I",
