  {
    "episode_id": 43939,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/3/43939.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 58,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, so we have a lot of different plans. You know, we're going to look at both things we can do. We have some grants working on funding that will be eventually getting an electric boat that can seat about up to eight people that will be able to use for a variety of pieces. everything from researchers coming in from out of town to our own staff, going out doing river monitoring, checking the health of the river throughout the season, also using a lot for our education programs. We do a lot of education outreach, summer camps, and we'll be able to get kids out on there and to get them more immersed and connected to the water. And everything from looking at the pollution that's in there to checking the health, the salinity levels, water temperature. So that'll be great, but you're all sorry, we can also partner with you know, tour groups that come through, you know, maybe at some point, you know, a kayak tour could pull up and could pop in and get like a quick lunch, a bite for lunch and then get continue on their tour. So I think there's a lot of different things we can do with this space and to have this for the community to be wonderful, to give more access to the water.",
