    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/3/43939.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 72,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, so the big plan is to have a building constructed on the Noyo Center, the Noyo Headlands, on the property that the Noyo Center currently owns, as 11 acres right by where you mentioned where the crow's nest is. It's going to be called the Ocean Science Center, and that's going to be our main goal to have a big museum, destination for people from the community as well as across the world we hope that is going to be developed and built to house our 73 foot blue whale bones skeleton that we hope to articulate soon and that will be kind of one of the main exhibits of that destination along with other marine exhibits. In order to do that, we're also planning on constructing what we're calling the La Bonitory, which is going to be a building that we'll build ahead of the Ocean Science Center so that we can actually have a place large enough that will allow us to articulate a 73-foot of Blue Whale Skeleton. So that's in the works now. We have a grant that is going to help us get pre-construction, get us there with permits and doing the final design, building designs for that facility. But yeah, that's gonna be a really big project and we've been working hard on that for a number of years.",
